title: art_context
---
// The Art dimension's people (`docs/planning/game/art-dimension.md`): the
// Curator who keeps the plaza's gate, and the painters of the plaza and the
// garden. One node per resident, named `art_<resident>`.
//
// The people here make things by hand, and they hate the gates the most:
// a gate brings strangers who make things fast and do not sign them. The
// Curator keeps the plaza's ring open anyway.
//
// The Curator knows of the colour gate in the garden and of its three
// pigments (the flags `art_pigment_cyan`, `art_pigment_magenta`,
// `art_pigment_yellow`), and says so; nothing here sets a flag.
//
// The four quarters (fiction, performance, music, teknis) have their own
// people below. The Author is the one writer of a flag here: the ending the
// player chooses (`art_ending_happy`, `art_ending_sad`) opens its door in the
// library of endings.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: art_curator
---
<<if condition("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_cyan") and condition("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_magenta") and condition("world.flag_set", "art_pigment_yellow")>>
The Curator: All three. Cyan, magenta, yellow. Every colour in this world is some of each.
The Curator: The gate at the end of the garden will know them. Go and see what nobody finished.
<<else>>
The Curator: Welcome to the Painter's Plaza. Mind the easel. It is the gate, and it is also an easel. We are not good at one thing at a time.
<<endif>>
-> What is this place?
    The Curator: The Art dimension. Here a thing that somebody makes keeps going after they stop. Doodles walk. Statues think about it. Ink has opinions.
    The Curator: Up is the sketchbook. Down is the ink. East is the garden, where the statues are not finished. None of it is.
-> Is it all paint?
    The Curator: Paint is only the part you can hang on a wall. West are the Story Stacks, where the books are. Over them is the Amphitheatre, where people pretend for a living.
    The Curator: East of the cliffs is the Resonant Reach. Music lives there, and it is loud about it. Under everything, past the ink, the makers work in the Deeps.
    The Curator: A song, a play, a story, a well-made chair. Each is somebody telling somebody else a thing that does not fit in words. That is all art is.
-> Why does everybody glare at the gate?
    The Curator: Because of what comes through it. Strangers who make a thousand pictures before lunch and sign none of them.
    The Curator: Here a mark means somebody meant it. They are afraid that a mark will stop meaning anything.
    The Curator: I keep the gate open. A shut door is a statement too, and I did not want to make that one.
-> And you? What do you make?
    The Curator: Room. A curator makes room for a thing, and then gets out of the way of it. Most of my work is standing still in the right place.
    The Curator: You are a made thing too. I can tell by the way you look at the frames. You want to know who meant you.
-> The gate in the garden. The one with the shapes.
    The Curator: The colour gate. Three doors: a circle, a star, a triangle. Each wants its own pigment.
    The Curator: Cyan is high, where the pages end. Magenta is low, where the ink falls. Yellow is on the head that nobody finished.
    The Curator: Bring the three. A painter can make anything out of three.
-> Goodbye.
    The Curator: Look slowly on the way out. Things here like to be seen.
===

title: art_rob_boss
---
Rob Boss: Well, hi. Come stand over here. You can see the whole cliff from here, and the cliff can see you.
-> What are you painting?
    Rob Boss: A happy little mountain. And over here, he needs a friend. Everybody needs a friend. There, a happy little tree.
    Rob Boss: Now, in this dimension the tree may get up and walk off. That is all right. We let it go.
-> Do the doodle imps bother you?
    Rob Boss: Those are just mistakes that got their legs. We don't make mistakes here. We make doodle imps.
    Rob Boss: You give them a little room, they run along. You chase them, you get a whole margin of them.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Rob Boss: It brings a lot of folks who paint a thousand mountains a minute. Their mountains are fine. They are all the same fine.
    Rob Boss: I don't mind. I just keep painting mine, one at a time, and I talk to it while I do. Nobody can take the talking.
-> I'll let you work.
    Rob Boss: You go on. And hey: whatever you make today, you made it. That is the whole trick.
===

title: art_pablo_peckasso
---
Pablo Peckasso: Coo. Stop. Turn your head. No, the other way. Now both ways at once.
-> I can't look both ways at once.
    Pablo Peckasso: That is your trouble, not mine. I have an eye on each side. I see the front of you and the side of you, and I paint both. It is only fair to you.
-> Why are you a pigeon?
    Pablo Peckasso: Why are you a robot? We are what we were made. Then we make something else. That is the order of things.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Pablo Peckasso: Good artists copy. Great artists steal. The things that come through that gate do neither. They average.
    Pablo Peckasso: An average of every face is no face. I have painted it. It is in my blue period. Everything is blue in it, and nothing looks back.
-> Can I see the painting of me?
    Pablo Peckasso: You may. It has three eyes. You have two, but I have been looking at you from two sides, so the third is a gift.
-> Goodbye.
    Pablo Peckasso: Coo. Come back when you have another side to show me.
===

title: art_frida_collage
---
Frida Collage: Stand still. You have a piece I want: a bolt, there, by your ear. No? Keep it, then. I will remember it.
-> What do you make?
    Frida Collage: My own face, out of the scraps of other pictures. The eyes from a saint, the mouth from a poster, the brows are mine.
    Frida Collage: A collage is honest. You can see where every piece came from. That is why I am not afraid of it.
-> The statues here move.
    Frida Collage: The ones that are not finished. A finished statue has nothing left to want. These want a back for their heads, and a reason.
    Frida Collage: Do not stand in front of one that is looking at you. Stand behind it. Behind it, it is still a block.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Frida Collage: It brings scraps. A great many scraps, from everywhere, with the names cut off.
    Frida Collage: I would use them if I knew whose they were. A scrap with no name is a stranger in my face.
-> Goodbye.
    Frida Collage: Go on. I have your bolt in my head now. It will be in a picture by Thursday.
===

title: art_edgar_allan_crow
---
Edgar Allan Crow: Once upon a midnight dreary... no. It is noon. It is always noon when I start, and midnight when I finish.
-> What are you writing?
    Edgar Allan Crow: A poem about a bird who says one word. Over and over. To a man who keeps asking him questions.
    Edgar Allan Crow: It is not about me. Everybody thinks it is about me.
-> Will you ever finish it?
    Edgar Allan Crow: Nevermore.
    Edgar Allan Crow: That is not an answer. It is the only word I have left this week.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Edgar Allan Crow: It opens on dark places, and things come through that nobody asked for. I approve of the atmosphere. I do not approve of the guests.
-> Goodbye.
    Edgar Allan Crow: Nevermore. That means "see you soon." Context is everything.
===

title: art_agatha_christly
---
Agatha Christly: Stop there. Where were you at a quarter past four?
-> I just got here.
    Agatha Christly: That is exactly what the murderer said in my last book. And in the one before that.
-> Who was murdered?
    Agatha Christly: Nobody yet. I like to have the suspect ready first. It saves a great deal of time.
    Agatha Christly: Mark my words: it will be the bookworm, in the stacks, with a page.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Agatha Christly: Every gate is a locked room with a way in. I have written eleven books about less.
-> Goodbye.
    Agatha Christly: Do not leave the dimension. You are not under suspicion. Yet.
===

title: art_the_author
---
The Author: Ah. A character. You walked in without a plot. Everybody does.
-> Who are you?
    The Author: I keep the endings. Every story in the stacks comes here at the end to be finished. Most of them do not want to be.
-> Write me an ending.
    The Author: Which kind? Think before you say. An ending changes what came before it.
    -> A happy one.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "art_ending_happy" true>>
        The Author: Easy to write, hard to believe. There. The gold door is yours. Everyone is paid and nobody learns anything.
    -> A sad one.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "art_ending_sad" true>>
        The Author: Hard to write, easy to believe. There. The blue door, up on the balcony. It is worth more. They always are.
    -> Not yet.
        The Author: Good. A story that knows its ending too early is only a list.
-> Why does everybody here hate the gate?
    The Author: Because what comes through it has read everything and meant none of it. A story needs somebody who meant it.
-> Goodbye.
    The Author: Go on, then. I will see you at the end.
===

title: art_ludwig_van_beathoven
---
Ludwig van Beathoven: WHAT?
-> I said hello.
    Ludwig van Beathoven: I CANNOT HEAR YOU. I CAN HEAR THE MUSIC. THAT IS THE IMPORTANT PART.
-> What are the chimes for?
    Ludwig van Beathoven: THREE CHIMES. DO, MI, SOL. STRIKE ALL THREE AND THE HALL OPENS ITS STAGE. A CHORD IS A DOOR, BOY. ROBOT. WHATEVER YOU ARE.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Ludwig van Beathoven: IT HUMS IN B FLAT. NOTHING GOOD WAS EVER WRITTEN IN THE KEY OF A DOOR.
-> Goodbye.
    Ludwig van Beathoven: DA DA DA DUMMM. THAT MEANS GOODBYE.
===

title: art_johann_sebastian_batch
---
Johann Sebastian Batch: Shh. The fugue is running. Four voices, one theme, no voice waits for another. It is very efficient.
-> What is a fugue?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: A theme, then the same theme a little later, then again, until they all run together and nobody is in charge. Very like a good workshop.
-> Why are the bars of the stage shut?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: They wait for the chord. The three chimes of the reach. Do, mi, sol. I would strike them myself, but I am in the middle of a process.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Johann Sebastian Batch: It brings strangers who make a thousand tunes an hour. I make one a week, for every week of my life. In the end mine add up to more.
-> Goodbye.
    Johann Sebastian Batch: Go well. Come back for the next run. It is the same fugue. That is the point.
===

title: art_marcel_mimeau
---
Marcel Mimeau: (He bows. He holds up one finger: wait. He takes a key out of the air and opens a door that is not there.)
-> What are you doing?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He points at you, then at the stage, then mimes a crowd that claps. He wipes a tear.)
-> Do you ever talk?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He opens his mouth. He thinks about it. He shuts it again, very slowly, and shrugs a huge shrug.)
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Marcel Mimeau: (He walks into a wall that is not there. He feels along it. He finds no door in it. He is sad about this for a long time.)
-> Goodbye.
    Marcel Mimeau: (He waves. He keeps waving after you leave. You can feel it.)
===

title: art_will_shakesbeard
---
Will Shakesbeard: Good morrow, metal friend. What brings thee here, to stand upon the boards where players die?
-> Is that a real beard?
    Will Shakesbeard: The beard is real; the rest is all a play. I grew it for a part. The part is gone.
-> What is the audition?
    Will Shakesbeard: Step on the stage, and they will test thy nerve. The critics throw, the stagehands lift and shove. Survive the scene: the curtain will be drawn.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Will Shakesbeard: A door that every stranger may come through, and none of them can say a line by heart.
-> Goodbye.
    Will Shakesbeard: Parting is such sweet sorrow. Go. Exit, pursued by nobody in particular.
===

title: art_leonardo_da_vinchy
---
Leonardo da Vinchy: Hold still. No, keep moving. I am drawing how you move.
-> What are you making?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: A flying machine, a diving suit, a better bridge, and a portrait of a woman who will not stop smiling. All at once. None of them finished.
-> Why do you write backward?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: So that nobody can read my notebooks over my shoulder. Also, I am left-handed, and the ink does not smudge this way. Mostly the second one.
-> The pit of fire.
    Leonardo da Vinchy: The bridge is mine. Pull the lever by the crane and it swings in. I tested it many times. With sandbags. Mostly.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Leonardo da Vinchy: I would like to take it apart. I would like very much to take it apart.
-> Goodbye.
    Leonardo da Vinchy: Go. If you see a bird, look at its wings for me. I am behind on birds.
===

title: art_antoni_gaudy
---
Antoni Gaudy: Do not look for a straight line. There are none in nature, and there are none in my work. Nature and I agree.
-> What are you building?
    Antoni Gaudy: A cathedral. I started it a long time ago. It will be finished. Not by me. That is how a cathedral works.
-> Is anything here finished?
    Antoni Gaudy: The loom hall has a tapestry that is done, they say. I have not seen it. I do not trust a thing that is done.
-> What do you think of the gate?
    Antoni Gaudy: Three straight sides. Three. I would have let it melt a little.
-> Goodbye.
    Antoni Gaudy: Go slowly. Everything worth making takes longer than a life.
===
